  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe previous approach relies on incremental local optimization phases that optimize a barrier function. However, this method is local in nature and may get trapped in suboptimal regions. The key bottleneck is its inability to efficiently explore the entire solution space. The new approach overcomes this by combining population-based optimization (Differential Evolution) for global exploration, which uses a repair mechanism to maintain constraints, and a multi-resolution strategy. This ensures we escape local optima by exploring diverse solutions across different grid resolutions while consistently enforcing the integral constraint. The objective function is augmented with an exponential barrier to handle the non-convexity of the problem, and the optimization proceeds in phases from low to high resolution to balance computational cost and solution quality.\n\nMathematical specification:\nVariables: \n- \\( h \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n} \\) where \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) for all \\( i \\)\n- Integral constraint: \\( \\sum h[i] \\, \\Delta x = 1 \\) (with \\( \\Delta x = 2/n \\))\n\nObjective:\nMinimize \\( C\u2085 = \\max_k (\\int h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx) \\) approximated using a smooth exponential barrier:\n\\[ \\text{obj} = \\max_k \\text{overlap}_k - \\lambda (\\sum h[i] \\Delta x - 1)^2 \\]\nplus a logarithmic barrier term:\n\\[ \\text{obj} += \\mu \\log(\\min(\\text{overlap}, \\epsilon)) \\]\nwhere \\( \\epsilon \\) is a small constant to avoid undefined logarithms.\n\nConstraints:\n- Box constraints: \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) for all \\( i \\)\n- Integral constraint: \\( \\sum h[i] \\Delta x = 1 \\) (handled by repair function)\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\n1. Given input `seed` and `budget_s`, initialize:\n   - `t0 = time.time()`\n   - Start resolution `n0 = 50`\n   - Generate initial population (size=20) of vectors in \\([0,1]^n0\\) using `initial_h_values` and random perturbations, ensuring integral constraint via repair.\n\n2. For each population member:\n   a. Repair to ensure \\( \\sum h = n0/2 \\) and \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\).\n   b. Evaluate \\( C\u2085 \\) (using correlation max) and integral cost.\n   c. Select best individual.\n\n3. If time budget is exceeded: return best candidate.\n\n4. Otherwise, ascend to next resolution:\n   - Set `n1 = 2 * n0` (doubles grid points).\n   - Interpolate best candidate from `n0` grid to `n1` points with periodic boundary condition.\n   - Repair and run DE optimization on new resolution.\n\n5. Phase I: Differential Evolution on `n0` grid:\n   - Objective function: converge barrier method.\n   - Repair mechanism: maintain constraints during DE mutation and crossover.\n   - Stop when function evaluations reach half budget.\n\n6. Phase II: Multi-start local optimization on best of Phase I for `n0`.\n   - Use L-BFGS-B with barrier function to fine-tune.\n   - Hyperparameters: `a=50`, `l=0.5`.\n   - Repair during local steps.\n\n7. Phase III: Resolution doubling (if time and n0 < 300) and repeat DE.\n\n8. Final polishing with grid interpolation and each solver's local optimizer.\n\nSearch choices and variations:\nWe use Differential Evolution with default settings but vary the population size (20 basis) and crossover probability (0.9). We dynamically adjust the penalty parameter `\u03bb` in \\([100, 1000]\\) based on the integral constraint violation. The repair mechanism is applied consistently during resampling and crossover.\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n- Total time: 50% initial DE, 30% multi-start local, 20% resolution doubling.\n- Interrupt DE if time is exceeded, use current best as fallback.\n- Repair: ensures integral constraint and bounds via exponentiation and threshold adjustment.\n- Internal timers: break phases at specified thresholds (e.g., DE phase evaluated up to 100 times).\n\nValidation:\n- Feasibility: \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) and \\( \\sum h \\Delta x = 1 \\).\n- Objective: compute \\( C\u2085 \\) using repeated correlation.\n- Output: return `(best_h, best_C5, final_n)`.\n\nReturns `(best_h, best_C5, final_n)` as the constrained solution.",
